{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c739","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0a986-16d4-7923-b79d-f784beb66015"},"sequence":503,"recorded_at":1780531400001001,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"f870b610-f41f-4227-a805-61cb8ebcb67d","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"f870b610-f41f-4227-a805-61cb8ebcb67d","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"f870b610-f41f-4227-a805-61cb8ebcb67d"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0a987-3c75-7621-870e-dbd1eccce0d9"},"task_id":"01a0a987-3c75-7621-870e-dbd1eccce0d9","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0a987-3c75-7621-870e-dbd1eccce0d9","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Documents/Codex/2026-09-15/new-chat-4/work/skills/skill-ios-testing-tools/agents/skills/ios-testing-tools/SKILL.md`.\n1|---\n2|name: ios-testing-tools\n3|description: |\n4|  iOS UI testing toolkit with screenshot validation. Use when:\n5|  (1) Setting up UI test infrastructure with Page Object pattern\n6|  (2) Creating accessibility identifiers with structured naming\n7|  (3) Writing UI tests with step-by-step screenshots\n8|  (4) Validating UI via screenshot comparison\n9|  (5) Writing snapshot tests with swift-snapshot-testing\n10|  (6) Comparing snapshot diffs with snapshotsdiff CLI\n11|  (7) Integrating with Allure for test reporting\n12|  (8) Organizing shared test identifiers between app and test targets\n13|  (9) Building physical iOS/iOS E2E harnesses with XCUITest runner-app marker synchronization\n14|  File types: Swift UI tests, XCUITest, xcresult, Allure reports, snapshot tests\n15|---\n16|\n17|# iOS UI Validation\n18|\n19|Write UI, add UI tests with structured markup, validate via screenshots.\n20|\n21|> **Repository:** [swift-ui-testing-tools](https://github.com/ivalx1s/swift-ui-testing-tools)\n22|>\n23|> **Before using this skill, check for updates:**\n24|> ```bash\n25|> cd ~/src/swift-ui-testing-tools && git pull\n26|> ```\n27|\n28|## Prerequisites\n29|\n30|**Before starting, verify required tools are installed:**\n31|\n32|```bash\n33|./Scripts/check-tools.sh\n34|```\n35|\n36|Required:\n37|- Xcode Command Line Tools (`xcodebuild`, `xcrun`, `xcresulttool`)\n38|- Swift 5.9+\n39|- iOS Simulator with available devices when the selected test platform is iOS\n40|\n41|## Test Platform Selection\n42|\n43|Pick the execution platform from the target declaration before running anything.\n44|\n45|- iOS app target or app package: run tests and runtime by default on iOS Simulator.\n46|- App target or app package on another declared platform: run against that declared platform.\n47|- If the app/package does not declare any platform, default to macOS.\n48|- Package module or multi-platform package: run unit and integration tests on macOS when macOS is supported.\n49|- If macOS is not supported, prefer iOS when iOS is declared.\n50|- If neither macOS nor iOS is available, use the first declared platform.\n51|- Use simulator destinations only for iOS-family runs. For macOS, run on macOS directly.\n52|- If a reusable iOS package is consumed through an app-local graph and standalone package schemes cannot resolve local dependencies, verify it through the app workspace/test host instead of treating the standalone package build as the product build.\n53|\n54|## Native macOS without UI tests\n55|\n56|When the user excludes UI tests, honor that scope: use Swift Testing for reducers, services and integration boundaries. Do not scaffold XCUITest, require screenshot extraction from unit-only runs, or launch iOS simulators. Use `Scripts/run-macos-package-tests.sh --package <CorePackage> --output <project>/.temp/macos-tests` from this repository. It preserves logs, JUnit output and the failure exit code. Hostless Core packages keep menu-bar app lifecycle out of the tests. For launchd controllers, test with a unique temporary service and harmless process; always unload it, including after failures. Never start production CI jobs as a unit-test side effect.\n57|\n58|A native menu-bar app needs native macOS UI, not the Designed for iPad destination. Build the app with `-destination 'platform=macOS'`, inspect its signed bundle and launch the app normally for manual smoke validation. Do not mistake local service state for GitHub online/busy state. Keep app-level runtime verification separate from automated UI tests.\n59|\n60|## Apple Silicon Mac iOS-App Runtime\n61|\n62|Use this path when the product app is an iOS app and the test environment needs a Mac-side peer or host process on Apple Silicon. This is **not** a separate macOS app target and not Catalyst. It is the existing iPhoneOS app running on the Mac as `Designed for iPad/iPhone`.\n63|\n64|First verify that Xcode exposes the destination:\n65|\n66|```bash\n67|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n68|xcodebuild \\\n69|  -workspace App.xcworkspace \\\n70|  -scheme App \\\n71|  -showdestinations\n72|```\n73|\n74|Look for a destination like:\n75|\n76|```text\n77|{ platform:macOS, arch:arm64, variant:Designed for [iPad,iPhone], name:My Mac }\n78|```\n79|\n80|Build the existing iOS app for that destination:\n81|\n82|```bash\n83|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n84|xcodebuild \\\n85|  -workspace App.xcworkspace \\\n86|  -scheme App \\\n87|  -destination 'platform=macOS,arch=arm64,variant=Designed for iPad' \\\n88|  build\n89|```\n90|\n91|Important limitations:\n92|\n93|- Do not create a separate macOS app target just to get a Mac-side peer. Use the existing iOS app on the Apple Silicon Mac destination unless the product explicitly needs native macOS UI.\n94|- `open Debug-iphoneos/App.app` is not a valid launch path for this runtime. macOS may report `incorrect executable format` because the product is still an iPhoneOS bundle.\n95|- `devicectl` targets physical devices; it does not reliably expose the Apple Silicon Mac `Designed for iPad` runtime as an install/launch device.\n96|- XCUITest cannot drive the Mac `Designed for iPad` destination. `xcodebuild test` can fail with `UI tests are not supported on My Mac (Designed for iPad)`.\n97|\n98|To launch the Mac-hosted iOS app from automation, ask Xcode to run the scheme through AppleScript. This uses the same run machinery as pressing Run in Xcode:\n99|\n100|```bash\n101|osascript <<'APPLESCRIPT'\n102|set workspacePath to POSIX file \"/absolute/path/App.xcworkspace\"\n103|tell application \"Xcode\"\n104|    activate\n105|    open workspacePath\n106|    set workspaceDocument to workspace document \"App.xcworkspace\"\n107|    repeat 120 times\n108|        if loaded of workspaceDocument is true then exit repeat\n109|        delay 0.5\n110|    end repeat\n111|    if loaded of workspaceDocument is false then error \"Xcode workspace did not finish loading\"\n112|    set actionResult to debug workspaceDocument scheme \"App\" run destination specifier \"platform=macOS,arch=arm64,variant=Designed for iPad\" skip building true\n113|    return id of actionResult\n114|end tell\n115|APPLESCRIPT\n116|```\n117|\n118|Stop the running action when the test environment is done:\n119|\n120|```bash\n121|osascript <<'APPLESCRIPT'\n122|tell application \"Xcode\"\n123|    set workspaceDocument to workspace document \"App.xcworkspace\"\n124|    stop workspaceDocument\n125|end tell\n126|APPLESCRIPT\n127|```\n128|\n129|For cross-runtime e2e, run the Mac-hosted iOS app as one endpoint, then run the actual XCUITest against the physical iPhone or Simulator endpoint:\n130|\n131|```bash\n132|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n133|xcodebuild \\\n134|  -workspace App.xcworkspace \\\n135|  -scheme AppUITests \\\n136|  -destination 'id=<physical-device-udid>' \\\n137|  -only-testing:AppUITests/SomeE2ETests/testPhoneSeesMacHostedPeer \\\n138|  test\n139|```\n140|\n141|Keep screenshots and assertions on the XCTest-driven endpoint. For the Mac-hosted iOS app, rely on app automation configuration, process checks, Xcode action status, logs, or manual permission approval when the OS prompts for Bluetooth, Local Network, or similar privacy access.\n142|\n143|## Connected Device Builds\n144|\n145|Use `ios-device-build` from this skill source repo when an iOS app should be built for every currently connected physical device without hard-coding an expected device list.\n146|\n147|The CLI has an explicit contract: always pass the Xcode source (`--workspace` or `--project`), `--scheme`, `--targets`, and `--derived-data-root`. Pass `--discovery` whenever `--targets` includes physical iPhones/iPads. Do not rely on inferred scheme names, inferred workspace/project names, or default discovery modes. The tool fails on missing required parameters so the agent can see exactly which assumption was wrong.\n148|\n149|The source repo path used by the local workflow is:\n150|\n151|```bash\n152|TOOLKIT=/Users/alexis/src/relux-works/skill-ios-testing-tools\n153|```\n154|\n155|### Physical Device Build Means Install\n156|\n157|When the user asks to build \"on\", \"for\", or \"to\" a physical iPhone/iPad, treat the request as **build a signed app bundle and install it on the target device** unless the user explicitly asks for compile-only validation.\n158|\n159|`xcodebuild build -destination 'platform=iOS,id=<udid>'` only proves that the app can compile and sign for that device class. It does not update the app installed on the phone. After a successful physical-device build, install the resulting `.app` with `devicectl device install app --device <udid> <path-to-app>` or a project-provided build/install wrapper, then verify the install command succeeded. Launch the app as a separate step only when the user asks to run it or when runtime validation is part of the task.\n160|\n161|Build only cable-connected physical iPhones/iPads:\n162|\n163|```bash\n164|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n165|$TOOLKIT/Scripts/ios-device-build.sh \\\n166|  --workspace App.xcworkspace \\\n167|  --scheme App \\\n168|  --discovery usb \\\n169|  --targets iphones \\\n170|  --derived-data-root .temp/device-builds\n171|```\n172|\n173|Build every physical iOS device Xcode sees over USB or Wi-Fi:\n174|\n175|```bash\n176|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n177|$TOOLKIT/Scripts/ios-device-build.sh \\\n178|  --project App.xcodeproj \\\n179|  --scheme App \\\n180|  --discovery usb,wifi \\\n181|  --targets iphones \\\n182|  --derived-data-root .temp/device-builds\n183|```\n184|\n185|Build physical iOS devices plus the Apple Silicon Mac `Designed for iPad/iPhone` destination:\n186|\n187|```bash\n188|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n189|$TOOLKIT/Scripts/ios-device-build.sh \\\n190|  --workspace App.xcworkspace \\\n191|  --scheme App \\\n192|  --discovery usb \\\n193|  --targets iphones,macbook \\\n194|  --mac-destination 'platform=macOS,arch=arm64,variant=Designed for iPad' \\\n195|  --derived-data-root .temp/device-builds\n196|```\n197|\n198|Build only the Apple Silicon Mac `Designed for iPad/iPhone` destination:\n199|\n200|```bash\n201|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n202|$TOOLKIT/Scripts/ios-device-build.sh \\\n203|  --workspace App.xcworkspace \\\n204|  --scheme App \\\n205|  --targets macbook \\\n206|  --mac-destination 'platform=macOS,arch=arm64,variant=Designed for iPad' \\\n207|  --derived-data-root .temp/device-builds\n208|```\n209|\n210|Rules:\n211|\n212|- `--discovery` is an option set for physical-device interfaces: `usb`, `wifi`, or `usb,wifi`.\n213|- `--discovery` is required only when `--targets` includes `iphones`.\n214|- `--targets` is a destination-type set: `iphones`, `macbook`, or `iphones,macbook`.\n215|- Prefer `--discovery usb` for stable cable-connected work; Wi-Fi destinations can be stale or unstable.\n216|- Missing individual devices do not fail the command; the CLI builds whatever matching destinations are discovered.\n217|- No matching destinations is a failure.\n218|- Every planned destination gets its own DerivedData and `xcodebuild.log` under the required `--derived-data-root`.\n219|- Use `--dry-run` first when debugging discovery.\n220|\n221|## Physical Device Runtime Logs\n222|\n223|When UI tests or runtime behavior are investigated on a physical iPhone, inspect the live device console instead of guessing from `xcodebuild` output alone.\n224|\n225|- Prefer `devicectl device process launch --console` for app-scoped runtime logs on a connected device.\n226|- Always set `DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer` before `xcrun` commands if the shell does not already point at Xcode.\n227|- Resolve the target device first with `xcrun xcdevice list`.\n228|- Use the device UDID in commands; names can be ambiguous.\n229|\n230|Recommended flow:\n231|\n232|```bash\n233|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n234|xcrun xcdevice list\n235|```\n236|\n237|```bash\n238|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n239|xcrun devicectl device process launch \\\n240|  --device <device-udid> \\\n241|  --terminate-existing \\\n242|  --console \\\n243|  <bundle-id>\n244|```\n245|\n246|Notes:\n247|- `--console` attaches stdout/stderr and waits for app exit, so use it for live runtime triage.\n248|- `--terminate-existing` avoids reading stale logs from an older app process.\n249|- This is the preferred path for investigating auth/network/runtime failures on device.\n250|- If you need broader system logs beyond one app process, fall back to Xcode Devices window or Console.app with the same physical device selected.\n251|\n252|## Simulator Runtime Logs\n253|\n254|When debugging simulator runtime behavior, use `simctl spawn` with unified logging and keep the raw stream in `.temp/` for later grepping.\n255|\n256|First resolve and boot the simulator:\n257|\n258|```bash\n259|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n260|xcrun simctl list devices available\n261|\n262|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n263|xcrun simctl boot <simulator-udid> 2>/dev/null || true\n264|\n265|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n266|xcrun simctl bootstatus <simulator-udid> -b\n267|```\n268|\n269|Start a task-scoped log stream before launching the app or UI test:\n270|\n271|```bash\n272|mkdir -p .temp/<task-id>\n273|\n274|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n275|xcrun simctl spawn <simulator-udid> \\\n276|  log stream \\\n277|  --style compact \\\n278|  --level debug \\\n279|  --predicate 'process == \"AppName\" OR subsystem CONTAINS \"AppName\" OR category CONTAINS \"Auth\" OR eventMessage CONTAINS \"SDKSSO\"' \\\n280|  > .temp/<task-id>/sim-runtime-01.log 2>&1 &\n281|\n282|echo $! > .temp/<task-id>/sim-runtime-01.pid\n283|```\n284|\n285|Launch the app or run the UI test while the stream is active:\n286|\n287|```bash\n288|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n289|xcrun simctl launch --terminate-running-process \\\n290|  <simulator-udid> \\\n291|  <bundle-id>\n292|```\n293|\n294|Search the captured stream with focused patterns:\n295|\n296|```bash\n297|rg -n \"SDKSSO|Auth|login\\\\.mts|client|scope|redirect|callback|error|failed|exception\" \\\n298|  .temp/<task-id>/sim-runtime-01.log\n299|```\n300|\n301|Stop the stream after triage:\n302|\n303|```bash\n304|kill \"$(cat .temp/<task-id>/sim-runtime-01.pid)\" 2>/dev/null || true\n305|```\n306|\n307|If the app writes file diagnostics, resolve the simulator data container and inspect it directly:\n308|\n309|```bash\n310|CONTAINER=\"$(DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n311|  xcrun simctl get_app_container <simulator-udid> <bundle-id> data)\"\n312|\n313|find \"$CONTAINER/Documents\" -maxdepth 4 -type f -print\n314|```\n315|\n316|Notes:\n317|- Use unified logs for SDK/framework logs, `os_log`, and app runtime events.\n318|- Use the app Documents container for explicit file diagnostics such as CSV logs.\n319|- Do not rely on `xcodebuild` output alone; it often misses the failing runtime line.\n320|- Keep logs under `.temp/` and name them with the task id plus a sequence number.\n321|\n322|## Physical Device Tunnel Recovery\n323|\n324|Use this playbook when Xcode or `devicectl` can see a physical iPhone, pairing succeeds, but developer operations fail with a tunnel error such as:\n325|\n326|```text\n327|The tunnel connection failed while the system tried to connect to the device.\n328|Domain: com.apple.dt.CoreDeviceError\n329|Code: 4\n330|--\n331|Domain: com.apple.dt.RemotePairingError\n332|Code: 4\n333|```\n334|\n335|Typical `devicectl device info details` symptoms:\n336|\n337|- `pairingState: paired`\n338|- `transportType: wired` or `localNetwork`\n339|- `tunnelState: disconnected`\n340|- `ddiServicesAvailable: false`\n341|- `devicectl device info lockState --device <udid>` fails with `RemotePairingError Code=4`\n342|\n343|First collect the state and compare with a known-good physical device if one is attached:\n344|\n345|```bash\n346|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n347|xcrun xcdevice list\n348|\n349|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n350|xcrun devicectl list devices\n351|\n352|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n353|xcrun devicectl device info details \\\n354|  --device <device-udid> \\\n355|  --verbose \\\n356|  --timeout 30 \\\n357|  --json-output .temp/device-details.json \\\n358|  --log-output .temp/device-details.log\n359|\n360|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n361|xcrun devicectl device info lockState \\\n362|  --device <device-udid> \\\n363|  --verbose \\\n364|  --timeout 30 \\\n365|  --json-output .temp/device-lockstate.json \\\n366|  --log-output .temp/device-lockstate.log\n367|```\n368|\n369|If the device charges but does not appear in `ioreg`, the Mac only sees power, not the USB data interface. Reboot the iPhone, unlock it, reconnect a known-good data cable/port, and accept the Trust prompt before spending time on Xcode caches:\n370|\n371|```bash\n372|ioreg -p IOUSB -l -w 0 | rg \"USB Serial Number|<device-udid-without-dashes>\"\n373|```\n374|\n375|If USB data and pairing are present but the tunnel still fails, use the iPhone-side Developer networking reset before clearing host caches:\n376|\n377|1. On the iPhone, open `Settings -> Developer -> Networking`.\n378|2. Enable `Network Link Conditioner`.\n379|3. If present, enable `Network Override`; it may be absent on some iPhone/iOS builds.\n380|4. Open `Responsiveness`.\n381|5. Run `Test Responsiveness` and wait for it to finish.\n382|6. Retry `devicectl device info lockState --device <device-udid>`.\n383|\n384|This can recover a stale Developer networking stack without rebooting, unpairing, or resetting trusted computers. A successful recovery usually changes `details` to:\n385|\n386|- `tunnelState: connected`\n387|- `ddiServicesAvailable: true`\n388|- `developerModeStatus: enabled`\n389|- a non-empty `tunnelIPAddress`\n390|\n391|If the issue persists after the Responsiveness test:\n392|\n393|- Disable `Multipath Networking` in `Settings -> Developer` if the setting exists.\n394|- Check for host VPN or network filter interfaces with `scutil --nwi` and `ifconfig | rg \"utun|ipsec|tap|tun\"`.\n395|- Restart CoreDevice host services and retry:\n396|\n397|```bash\n398|killall CoreDeviceService remoted 2>/dev/null || true\n399|\n400|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n401|xcrun devicectl manage ddis update --verbose --timeout 120\n402|```\n403|\n404|- If the iPhone OS is newer than the installed Xcode support, install a matching Xcode/device support bundle before continuing.\n405|- Only then consider heavier steps such as unpairing, resetting Location & Privacy/trusted computers, deleting Xcode/CoreDevice caches, or rebooting the Mac.\n406|\n407|## Physical iOS/iOS E2E Synchronization\n408|\n409|For paired physical-device tests where two iPhones must coordinate roles such as observing peer, controlled peer, project-specific discovery signals, project-specific state transitions, or session handoff, use reactive orchestration instead of fixed sleeps.\n410|\n411|Read [references/physical-ios-ios-e2e-sync.md](references/physical-ios-ios-e2e-sync.md) before implementing or debugging a two-device harness.\n412|\n413|For XCUITest runner-app marker files, host-side cross-device marker bridges, and screenshot/snapshot evidence contracts, also read [references/physical-ios-ios-runner-marker-sync.md](references/physical-ios-ios-runner-marker-sync.md).\n414|\n415|## Generalized WebSocket E2E Coordinator Sample\n416|\n417|Use the toolkit-local sample smoke to validate the reusable WebSocket coordinator, process peer runner, event log, receipt log, replay path, `acked` delivery barrier, JSON payload assertions, timestamp envelope, and deterministic artifact layout without any consumer product project or physical device.\n418|\n419|Run from the toolkit source repo:\n420|\n421|```bash\n422|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n423|./Scripts/run-e2e-sample-smoke.sh\n424|```\n425|\n426|The smoke writes artifacts under `.temp/e2e-sample/<session-id>/` and fails if the required events, receipts, peer markers, summary, or logs are missing.\n427|\n428|For physical devices, the XCUITest runner process must be able to open a TCP connection to the coordinator. Use global `coordinator.advertisedHost` when all peers share the same reachable host. Use peer-level `coordinatorHost` when different peers need different routes to the same coordinator; the runner keeps the global bind/port/path and injects a peer-specific `E2E_COORDINATOR_URL`.\n429|\n430|```yaml\n431|peers:\n432|  - name: peer-a\n433|    coordinatorHost: 192.168.50.10\n434|    launch:\n435|      kind: xctest\n436|      startWhen:\n437|        type: immediate\n438|```\n439|\n440|## Generalized Peer-Listener E2E Transport\n441|\n442|Use `coordinator.transport: peer-listener` when physical iOS peers cannot reach a Mac-hosted coordinator directly. The UI test peer starts a device-side TCP listener through `UITestE2EClient.fromEnvironment()`, the Mac runner starts `iproxy` for each device, and the Mac coordinator connects to every peer through host-local forwarded ports.\n443|\n444|This transport is project-neutral. Keep all product scenario semantics in the consumer project's UI tests and config. Do not add consumer-specific events, device names, bundle IDs, or business flow assumptions to this toolkit or skill.\n445|\n446|Consumer UI tests should import `IOSE2EPeerClient`, not `IOSE2ECoordinatorCore`, for generalized E2E coordination. Keep `UITestKit` for common UI helpers. Use `client.environment.peerNameValue` for scenario branching and `UITestE2EClient.publish` / `waitFor` for JSON event synchronization.\n447|\n448|Run the toolkit-local peer-listener smoke before attaching a product project:\n449|\n450|```bash\n451|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n452|./Scripts/run-e2e-peer-listener-sample-smoke.sh\n453|```\n454|\n455|Minimal config shape:\n456|\n457|```yaml\n458|coordinator:\n459|  transport: peer-listener\n460|\n461|peers:\n462|  - name: peer-a\n463|    launch:\n464|      kind: xctest\n465|      startWhen:\n466|        type: immediate\n467|    connection:\n468|      listenPort: 19131\n469|      connectHost: 127.0.0.1\n470|      connectPort: 18131\n471|      proxy:\n472|        kind: iproxy\n473|        udid: 00000000-0000000000000000\n474|```\n475|\n476|If a physical run fails before peer logs are written, first check CoreDevice state. A peer with `tunnelState: disconnected` or `ddiServicesAvailable: false` cannot reliably launch XCUITest even when `xcdevice list` says `available=true`.\n477|\n478|## Main Workflow\n479|\n480|1. **Write UI** with accessibility identifiers\n481|2. **Write UI tests** using Page Object pattern\n482|3. **Capture screenshots** at each step\n483|4. **Extract and review** screenshots to verify UI looks as expected\n484|5. **MANDATORY: Visually verify screenshots** - open and check that UI is rendered correctly\n485|\n486|## CRITICAL: Screenshot Verification\n487|\n488|After extracting screenshots, **ALWAYS open and visually inspect them** using the Read tool. Check for:\n489|\n490|- **Orientation issues** - UI should not be rotated/sideways\n491|- **Layout problems** - UI elements should be visible and properly positioned, not squished in corners\n492|- **Content visibility** - all expected UI elements should be present and readable\n493|- **Black screens** - indicates app didn't launch or UI didn't render\n494|\n495|Common issues and fixes:\n496|- **Rotated UI in landscape frame**: Force portrait in PageManager: `XCUIDevice.shared.orientation = .portrait`\n497|- **Empty/black screenshots**: Check app launch, add delays if needed\n498|- **Partial UI**: Scroll or adjust viewport before screenshot\n499|\n500|## Core Components\n(more lines below)","final_result":true}}}
